;; The deployment values the live gate uses. Not code, because none of it is a
;; property of this repository: which authorities a machine may reach, which
;; keys it will accept from them, and which block it asks for are things an
;; operator decides and a fleet changes without recompiling anything.
;;
;; `kbb -M:cloud-live check` reads this. `kbb -M:cloud-live pin <origin>`
;; MEASURES a peer key and prints it; it does not write here. Putting a
;; measured key into this file is a person deciding to trust it, and that
;; decision is the one thing a tool must not make on its own.
{:aiueos.cloud-live/version 1

 ;; ── who this machine may reach ────────────────────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; Three hosts, not two. ADR-0041 names two authorities; the third is here
 ;; because the murakumo alias entry names it, and admitting it is an explicit
 ;; operator decision rather than something the resolver got to make. Delete
 ;; the entry and the gate's model-resolve leg refuses with
 ;; `:resolved-endpoint-not-allowed`, and the inference leg reports that it had
 ;; no admitted endpoint to ask -- which is ADR-0042's re-check working, not a
 ;; fault.
 ;;
 ;; That third host is also the one that answers. `api.murakumo.cloud` fronts
 ;; the Anthropic-shaped `/v1/messages` and demands a token;
 ;; `infer.murakumo.cloud` serves the OpenAI-shaped `/v1/chat/completions` and
 ;; answered 200 with a real completion on 2026-08-22 with no credential at
 ;; all. Both were measured; neither is assumed.
 :aiueos.policy/net-allow #{"kotobase.net" "api.murakumo.cloud" "infer.murakumo.cloud"}

 :aiueos.cloud/storage-origin "https://kotobase.net"
 :aiueos.cloud/inference-origin "https://api.murakumo.cloud"
 :aiueos.cloud/model-alias "murakumo-main"
 :aiueos.cloud/liveness-path "/ready"

 ;; Off, deliberately. If the alias entry ever stops carrying an endpoint, the
 ;; gate must say `:alias-unresolved` rather than quietly reach the origin it
 ;; was configured with. Turning this on is a decision someone makes in this
 ;; file, and the receipt then reports `:endpoint-source :configured-origin`.
 :aiueos.cloud/endpoint-from-origin? false

 ;; ── the keys, and how they were measured ──────────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; SHA-256 of the leaf certificate's DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo, **bound to the
 ;; host it was measured from**. Measured 2026-08-21 by two independent paths
 ;; that agreed:
 ;;
 ;;   openssl s_client -servername H -connect H:443 </dev/null \
 ;;     | openssl x509 -pubkey -noout | openssl pkey -pubin -outform DER \
 ;;     | openssl dgst -sha256
 ;;
 ;;   kbb -M:cloud-live pin https://H
 ;;
 ;; Until 2026-08-22 this was a flat set and the host each key came from lived
 ;; in a comment. That meant any one of these three keys was accepted from any
 ;; of these three hosts: an attacker able to answer for one of them could
 ;; answer for all three, and the receipt would have said ADMITTED. The mapping
 ;; is now the data structure, and presenting one host's key for another is
 ;; refused as `:peer-pinned-to-other-host` -- the one refusal here that cannot
 ;; be a rotation.
 ;;
 ;; ## WHEN THE EDGE ROTATES, WHICH IT WILL
 ;;
 ;; All three hosts are Cloudflare-fronted, so the leaf key is the edge's and
 ;; changes on its schedule. A person does this, in this file:
 ;;
 ;;   1. `kbb -M:cloud-live pin https://<host>` measures the new key. It
 ;;      prints MEASURED, NOT TRUSTED and writes nothing: deciding to trust a
 ;;      key is a person's act and a tool must not make it.
 ;;   2. Move that host's current `:pins` into `:previous`, put the measured
 ;;      key in `:pins`, and set `:accept-previous-until-ms` to when the old
 ;;      key should stop working (epoch millis).
 ;;   3. Nothing else. The clock retires the old key -- `grant.anchors/usable-anchors`
 ;;      decides which keys work today, the same function the release-borne
 ;;      anchor plane uses, so there is one implementation of the overlap rule
 ;;      and not two.
 ;;
 ;; The window is why this is worth doing at all rather than editing one line:
 ;; a Cloudflare edge mid-rotation serves the old key from some POPs and the
 ;; new key from others, so a client that accepts exactly one of them goes red
 ;; on a healthy authority. During the window both are admitted and the receipt
 ;; says `:window :open`. After it, the retired key is refused as
 ;; `:peer-pin-expired` -- which names a schedule, not an attack.
 ;;
 ;; This is NOT the signed, sequenced distribution of ADR-0045/0046, and should
 ;; not be described as one. That path exists for a device receiving anchors
 ;; over a network it cannot yet judge; it needs a publisher, root keys and a
 ;; release, and an operator workstation running this gate has none of them.
 ;; Here the file IS the trusted channel, and what the anchors plane lends is
 ;; its rotation vocabulary and its overlap rule.
 :aiueos.cloud/trust-anchors
 {"kotobase.net"
  {:pins #{"50602ad366823fcf5274a7c917baa4fd24b9de4fd15635ff501177c83d05473e"}
   :measured "2026-08-21"
   :fronted-by "cloudflare"}

  "api.murakumo.cloud"
  {:pins #{"ec7f258fc32457d84295f081a479910ee9b5250d34158fcf3c92eafb9dbd1af1"}
   :measured "2026-08-21"
   :fronted-by "cloudflare"}

  ;; Named by the murakumo-main alias entry's :endpoint field; it is the host
  ;; the inference leg actually POSTs to. Delete this entry and the TLS
  ;; handshake for that leg fails, REFUSED with :host-not-pinned -- which is
  ;; the pin doing its job, and is why the key is written down next to the date
  ;; and method it was measured by.
  "infer.murakumo.cloud"
  {:pins #{"014bccd86fa34b2a9dbc410b5d27e60e46fb938cc9c9b77058c567cc4b997038"}
   :measured "2026-08-21"
   :fronted-by "cloudflare"}}

 ;; No rotation is in progress: every host above has :pins and no :previous, so
 ;; every window reads :none in the receipt. A rotation looks like this, and is
 ;; here as the worked example the step above refers to:
 ;;
 ;;   "kotobase.net"
 ;;   {:pins     #{"<the key `cloud-live pin` just measured>"}
 ;;    :previous #{"50602ad366823fcf5274a7c917baa4fd24b9de4fd15635ff501177c83d05473e"}
 ;;    :accept-previous-until-ms 1798761600000   ; 2027-01-01T00:00:00Z
 ;;    :measured "2026-12-18"}

 ;; This deployment knows which host each key came from, so it refuses to run
 ;; on a policy that does not. Without it a flat set would be accepted -- marked
 ;; :unbound on every verdict, but accepted -- and this file would be able to
 ;; regress to the weaker shape by an edit nobody noticed. A device booted from
 ;; a release-borne anchor set has no such choice: that document carries a
 ;; device-wide pin list with no host field, which is why the flat shape is
 ;; accepted at all.
 :aiueos.cloud/require-host-bound-anchors? true

 ;; ── what the gate asks for ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; A CID, not a literal in code: which block this machine reads to prove it
 ;; can read is a deployment fact, and someone storing a more interesting one
 ;; should be able to point the gate at it by editing this line.
 ;;
 ;; Today it is the raw CIDv1 of the empty byte string
 ;; (sha256 = e3b0c442...b855). Unglamorous and exactly right for the purpose:
 ;; the gateway returns 200 with zero bytes, and `admit-block` still has to
 ;; hash what arrived and agree with the CID. A digest check that passes on
 ;; nothing is the same check as one that passes on a megabyte.
 :aiueos.cloud-live/read-cid
 "bafkreihdwdcefgh4dqkjv67uzcmw7ojee6xedzdetojuzjevtenxquvyku"

 ;; A well-formed CID the store does not hold. Measured 404 `not stored` on
 ;; 2026-08-21. The gate requires the refusal: if this ever returns 200, the
 ;; negative leg fails, because a negative that cannot fail is decoration.
 :aiueos.cloud-live/absent-cid
 "bafkreicjlihajri6k5g4n66xvq3xsffb45qddog7q2wpolck5kddammoey"

 ;; ── the write, for whoever holds the token ────────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; `kbb -M:cloud-live write` sends these bytes under this CID. The CID is
 ;; the sha2-256 raw CIDv1 of exactly the text below, so the store's own digest
 ;; check (422 on mismatch) and `admit-write-payload`'s must agree.
 ;; Verified 404 on 2026-08-21, so it is a clean target rather than a re-put.
 :aiueos.cloud-live/write-cid
 "bafkreibbrbij73q5rtrzldvp7mdwilmcap3ykxushndsvcsetga44sh5xa"
 :aiueos.cloud-live/write-text "aiueos live gate 2026-08-21\n"

 ;; ── credentials: named here, held nowhere ─────────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; The gate reads these environment variables and, if one is set, sends its
 ;; value as a header. It never prints one, never writes one down, and never
 ;; defaults one. A leg whose variable is unset is reported UNMEASURED -- not
 ;; skipped quietly, and never passed.
 ;;
 ;; kotobase's block plane wants `Authorization: Bearer <token>` compared
 ;; string-equal against an operator secret. It is NOT the CACAO plane; CACAO
 ;; is how a tenant authenticates to the datom surface, and a `CACAO ...`
 ;; header fails this gate's `Bearer ` prefix test before any verifier sees it.
 :aiueos.cloud-live/credentials
 {:inference {:env "AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_TOKEN" :header "authorization" :prefix "Bearer "}
  :write {:env "KOTOBASE_ARCHIVE_TOKEN" :header "authorization" :prefix "Bearer "}}

 ;; The inference leg's request. Short prompt on purpose: the gate is asking
 ;; whether a completion comes back at all, and a long prompt would make the
 ;; answer depend on the model's mood.
 ;;
 ;; The budget is NOT short, and that is the interesting part. This model emits
 ;; `reasoning_content` before it emits an answer, so a small ceiling produces a
 ;; 200 carrying `content: ""` and `finish_reason: "length"` -- a response that
 ;; arrived, parsed, and contains no completion. Measured 2026-08-22: at
 ;; max_tokens 8 exactly that; at 256, `finish_reason: "stop"` and "pong" after
 ;; 152 completion tokens. 512 is that with headroom.
 ;;
 ;; `grant.cloud/admit-inference` refuses the empty case as :completion-empty
 ;; either way -- the budget is here so the gate can go green when the fleet is
 ;; healthy, not so the refusal stops working.
 :aiueos.cloud-live/messages [{:role "user" :content "Reply with the single word: pong"}]
 :aiueos.cloud-live/max-tokens 512

 ;; Generously long, and deliberately so. `aiueos.provider.cloud`'s default
 ;; request timeout is 15s, which is right for fetching a block and wrong for
 ;; waiting on a 27B model with one production slot: measured 2026-08-22, the
 ;; same request took 23s and 56s on two consecutive runs of a busy fleet.
 ;;
 ;; A timeout that trips on a slow-but-healthy authority reports UNMEASURED,
 ;; and the gate cannot tell that apart from an endpoint that is gone. Being
 ;; impatient here would not make the gate stricter; it would make it blind.
 :aiueos.cloud-live/inference-timeout-ms 180000}
